<s>Upananda</s>, out of excessive greed, entered the rains-retreat at two monasteries, [thinking]: 'I shall obtain the shares from both.' When the rains were over, he sought his share but did not obtain it from either one. He reproached the lodging-assigning <s>bhikṣu</s>s. The <s>bhikṣu</s>s reported this matter to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said: 'One who has spent the rains there should be given his share.' The <s>bhikṣu</s>s gave him shares from both monasteries. The Blessed One said: 'It should not be given from both monasteries; they should give from one.' Other <s>bhikṣu</s>s complained, reproached, and protested: 'He spent the rains at both monasteries — why is he given from only one?' The <s>bhikṣu</s>s reported this matter to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said: 'Half should be given from each of the two monasteries. But a <s>bhikṣu</s> should not enter the rains at two monasteries; if he does, he commits a transgression.'
A certain householder had two monasteries built — one for forest-dwelling [monks] and a second for village-dwelling [monks]. It was his custom to cover each rains-dwelling <s>bhikṣu</s> with a cloth. He took the cloths and set out for the forest monastery. <s>Upananda</s> heard: 'Shares are being distributed at the forest monastery.' He instructed <s>Nanda</s>: 'If shares are distributed here, you shall take them.' Having said this, he hastily set out for the forest monastery. The householder saw him and reflected: 'Judging by his haste, he has gone to the forest monastery. What is appropriate here? Let me first distribute at the village monastery; afterward I shall give there.' He turned back and began distributing the cloths. <s>Nanda</s> extended his hand. The householder gave a cloth. He extended his hand again. The householder said: 'Noble one, a cloth has been given to you. Why do you extend again?' He said:

children.text:
) teṣu sūtreṣv anāthapiṇḍado gṛhapatir anāthapiṇḍado gṛhapatir iti, nāham tvā jāne nāpi gṛham; anāthapiṇḍado gṛhapatiḥ saṃlakṣayati: aprakṛtijño 'yaṃ bhikṣur dūrāgataḥ, dhruvaṃ na māṃ vetti ḥnāpi mama gṛhamḥ; kim atra prāptakālaṃ bhagavantam ālokayāmi yāvac ca śrāvastī yāvac ca jetavanam atrāntarāt parikramaṇakāṃ kārayāmi iti viditvā yena bhagavāṃs tenopasaṃkrāntaḥ; upasaṃkramya bhagavataḥ pādau śirasā vanditvā ekānte niṣaṇṇaḥ; ekāntaniṣaṇṇo 'nāthapiṇḍado gṛhapatir bvhagavantam idam avocat: āgantukā bhadanta bhikṣava agocarakuśalā piṇḍapātam aṭantaḥ kālātikramatayā kuḍyamūlaṃ niśritya bhaktakṛtyaṃ kurvanti; aśraddhā anenāṅgenāryakān dhvaṃsayisyanti; tad yadi bhagavān anujānīyād ahaṃ yāvac ca śrāvastī yāvac ca jetavanaṃ atrāntarāt parikramaṇakāṃ kārayeyam iti; yasya yatra velā bhaviṣyati sa tatra piṇḍapātaṃ paribhokṣyate iti; bhagavān āha: tasmād gṛhapate 'nujānāmi kāraya iti; tena yāvac ca śrāvastī yāvac ca jetavanam atrāntarāt parikramaṇakā kāritā; yasya bhikṣor
yatra velā bhavati sa tatra bhaktakṛtyaṃ karoti
  apareṣāṃ bhikṣūṇāṃ pānīyavighāto bhavati; anāthapiṇḍadena gṛhapatinā tatra kūpaḥ kāritaḥ trikaṭukasya triphalāyāś ca śakaṭabhāra utkṣiptaḥ; tat pānīyaṃ svādu saṃpannaṃ sarvalokaprakhyātam; janakāyaḥ śrutvā āgataḥ; bhikṣavo vārayanti; śrāvastīnivāsinaḥ: āryā vayaṃ yuṣmākaṃ sarvopakaraṇaiḥ pratyupasthitaḥ, yūyaṃ sarvalokasādhāraṇam pānīyaṃ vārayatha iti; etat prakaraṇaṃ bhikṣavo bhagavata ārocayanti; bhagavān āha: na nivārayitavyā iti; mātṛgrāmo bhikṣūṇāṃ pānīyaṃ yācate; bhikṣavo nānuprayacchanti; (

) tat pānīyaṃ yācate; yo 'pi tasyām avekṣāvān pratibandhacittaḥ; nivāryamāṇo 'py acchinnadhārayā dadāti evaṃ sāpi nirīkṣate, pibati, bata pānīyaṃ viṣamam gatam, kālagatā; akpārthā bhikṣavo 'vadhyāyanti kṣipanti vivācayanti; etat prakaraṇaṃ bhikṣavo bhagavata ārocayanti; asyām utpattau bhagavān āha: yā strīr bhikṣor antike avekṣāvatī bhavati pratibaddhacittā bhavati tasyā svayaṃ na dātavyam; na ca mātṛgrāmasya acchinnayā dhārayā pānīyaṃ dātavyam; antarāntarā vāridhārā cchettavyā vaktavyā vā añjaliṃ pūrayitvā piba iti, anyathā sātisāraḥ
  tena khalu samayena ye 'grapiṇḍapātaṃ jetavane pratipādayanti te tatraiva dātum ārabdhāḥ; ṣaḍvargikaiḥ śrutam; te parikramaṇikāṃ gatvāvasthitāḥ; yena ānīyate agarapiṇḍapātas taṃ pṛcchanti; yadi pāyaso bhavati pātram upanāmayanti; atha pānīyapeyā bhavati, kathayanti: mahardhikaḥ saṃghaḥ, saṃghaḥ sa mahānubhāvaḥ, saṃghāyānuprayacchata, evaṃ khaṇḍakhādyakādi yojayitavyam; alpārthā bhikṣavo 'vadhyāyanti kṣipanti vivācayanti; etat prakaraṇam bhikṣavo bhagavata ārocayanti; asyām utpattau bhagavān āha: na bhikṣuṇā parakīye lābhe saṃnipatitavyam: saṃnipatati, sātisāro bhavati; uktaṃ bhagavatā: na bhikṣuṇā parakīye lābhe saṃnipatitavyam iti
  bhikṣavaḥ kenacit karaṇīyena vihārāntaraṃ gacchanti; teṣāṃ tatra gatānāṃ velā āsannā bhavati; kaukṛtyān na paribhuñjate; etat prakaraṇam bhikṣavo bhagavata ārocayanti; asyām utpattau bhagavān āha: saṃcintya na gantavyam anyathā gatānāṃ yadi velā bhavati bhoktavyam, nātra kaukṭrtyaṃ karaṇīyam iti (

children.translated_text:
) She asked for that water. He too was attentive toward her, with an attached mind. Though warned, he poured an unbroken stream [of water]. She too gazed at him and drank. Alas, the water went down wrong [and she choked]; she died. <s>Bhikṣu</s>s of few desires complained, reproached, and protested. The <s>bhikṣu</s>s reported this matter to the Blessed One. On this occasion the Blessed One said: 'When a woman is attentive toward a <s>bhikṣu</s> and has an attached mind, he should not give to her himself. And water should not be given to women in an unbroken stream. The stream of water should be broken intermittently, or she should be told: "Fill your cupped hands and drink." Otherwise, he commits a transgression.'
At that time, those who delivered the choicest alms-food to the <s>Jetavana</s> began [distributing] right there [at the rest-pavilion]. The group of six (<s>ṣaḍvargika</s>) heard about this. They went and stood at the rest-pavilion. They would ask the one who was bringing the choicest alms-food [what it was]. If it was milk-rice, they would hold out their bowls. But if it was watery gruel, they would say: 'The <s>Saṃgha</s> is of great powers, the <s>Saṃgha</s> is of great majesty — give it to the <s>Saṃgha</s>.' And so the same should be understood for sweets and so forth. <s>Bhikṣu</s>s of few desires complained, reproached, and protested. The <s>bhikṣu</s>s reported this matter to the Blessed One. On this occasion the Blessed One said: 'A <s>bhikṣu</s> should not intrude upon another's alms. If he does, he commits a transgression.' It was said by the Blessed One: 'A <s>bhikṣu</s> should not intrude upon another's alms.'
When <s>bhikṣu</s>s went to another monastery on some business, mealtime arrived while they were there. Out of scruple, they did not eat. The <s>bhikṣu</s>s reported this matter to the Blessed One. On this occasion the Blessed One said: 'One should not go [to another monastery] with this intention. But if one has gone for other reasons and mealtime occurs, one should eat; no scruple should be felt about this.'
